Which directory the fingerprint HAL is handed, settled offline
2026-09-23. Host-side. Device: EDL (05c6:9008, port 3-3). Nothing here touched the device.
================================================================================

The question this settles: biometryd hands the HAL a store path, the HAL access(W_OK)es it, and a
failure there returns SYS_EINVAL with NO log line (docs 83). So which path, and does it exist?
Neither half had been answered from the real images before this round.

1. the source, read out of the halium checkout
================================================================================

$ sed -n '586,598p' /mnt/data/halium-zl1-build/halium/biometryd/src/biometry/devices/android.cpp
      hybris_fp_instance_{hybris_fp_instance}
{
    biometry::util::AndroidPropertyStore store;
    UHardwareBiometryRequestStatus ret = SYS_OK;
    std::string api_level = store.get("ro.product.first_api_level");
    if (api_level.empty())
        api_level = store.get("ro.build.version.sdk");
    if (atoi(api_level.c_str()) <= 27)
        ret = u_hardware_biometry_setActiveGroup(hybris_fp_instance, 0, (char*)"/data/system/users/0/fpdata/");
    else
        ret = u_hardware_biometry_setActiveGroup(hybris_fp_instance, 0, (char*)"/data/vendor_de/0/fpdata/");
    if (ret != SYS_OK)
        printf("setActiveGroup failed: %s\n", IntToStringRequestStatus(ret).c_str());

-- and `store.get` is not the Android property area, it is a FORK AND EXEC of an absolute path:

$ sed -n '22,35p' /mnt/data/halium-zl1-build/halium/biometryd/src/biometry/util/property_store.cpp
#include <core/posix/exec.h>

std::string biometry::util::AndroidPropertyStore::get(const std::string& key) const
{
    core::posix::ChildProcess getprop = core::posix::exec("/usr/bin/getprop", {key}, {}, core::posix::StandardStream::stdout);
    std::string value; getprop.cout() >> value;

    auto result = getprop.wait_for(core::posix::wait::Flags::untraced);

    if (result.status == core::posix::wait::Result::Status::exited && result.detail.if_exited.status == core::posix::exit::Status::success)
        return value;

    throw std::out_of_range{key};
}

   `/usr/bin/getprop` is the UT-side binary, and the v63 boot hook replaces it with a /bin/sh stub on
   every boot (docs 50, 93 -- the same stub that makes docs 82's two custom.location.* levers dead).
   So both reads return NOTHING, api_level stays "", atoi("") is 0, and the <=27 branch is taken.

2. the two values, from the images rather than from the device
================================================================================

$ sudo mount -o ro,noload,loop /mnt/data/zl1-backups/2026-06-07-adb-root-staged/vendor.img /mnt/vendor-ro
$ sudo grep -E 'first_api_level|version.sdk|board.platform|product.board' /mnt/vendor-ro/build.prop
ro.board.platform=msm8996
ro.product.board=msm8996
ro.product.first_api_level=23

   ro.product.first_api_level = 23  ->  atoi = 23 <= 27  ->  /data/system/users/0/fpdata/
   (ro.hardware is NOT in this build.prop, which is why hw_get_module falls through to
    ro.product.board=msm8996 -- the variant order doc 98 reads out of the library.)

   **So the device would say the same branch the broken read says.** The two do not merely agree on
   this device by luck: 23 and "" both land on <=27. Nothing here depends on that staying true --
   the probe now prints both, and says so when they disagree.

3. and does that directory exist? Asked of the partition image, not of the device
================================================================================

$ sudo mount -o ro,noload,loop /mnt/data/zl1-backups/2026-09-16-recovery-supplement/userdata.img /mnt/udata-ro
$ sudo ls -ld /mnt/udata-ro/system/users/0 /mnt/udata-ro/system/users/0/fpdata \
                /mnt/udata-ro/vendor_de/0/fpdata /mnt/udata-ro/gf_data
ls: cannot access 'system/users/0/fpdata': No such file or directory
ls: cannot access 'gf_data': No such file or directory
drwx------. 2 1000 1000 4096 Apr 25  1970 system/users/0
drwx------. 2 1000 1000 4096 Apr 25  1970 vendor_de/0/fpdata

   * /data/system/users/0            EXISTS   drwx------ system:system  -- and EMPTY (no fpdata)
   * /data/system/users/0/fpdata     MISSING  <- what biometryd passes, and what the HAL access()es
   * /data/vendor_de/0/fpdata        EXISTS   drwx------ system:system  -- and EMPTY
   * /data/gf_data                   MISSING  <- the innermost Goodix HAL's own store (docs 98)

   This is the first time these have been checked against a real image, and it settles two things:
   doc 83's "the EINVAL is a missing directory" is confirmed (the directory really is absent, in an
   image taken from a device that had booted many times), and doc 98's second store is absent too --
   so the two silences are two REAL absences, not two readings of one.
   The 26 GiB image mounts read-only with `noload`; nothing was written to it or to any device.

4. the defect this round found in the instrument: it decided from the wrong process's read
================================================================================

   The probe's section 2 asked the CONTAINER (`nsenter -t $A -p -- /system/bin/getprop ...`) and
   decided from that. biometryd does not read the container's properties -- it execs the UT-side
   /usr/bin/getprop. On the real device both answers are <=27, so the old code was right by
   coincidence. It stops being a coincidence the moment the UT getprop answers anything >27, and on a
   device whose first_api_level is >27 with a WORKING getprop it would name the wrong path with full
   confidence -- and section 6 writes exactly one directory, with an undo for that one.

   Demonstrated rather than argued. The harness can now give the UT-side getprop an answer, and the
   scenario is "container says 29, the real port's stub is in place":

$ sh /tmp/zl1-hw/scripts/host/zl1-loc-fp-selftest.sh      # the PRE-FIX probe
  90:FAIL  it says WHY biometryd lands on <=27 (its own read answers nothing), not why the device would
  166:FAIL  and states that biometryd's OWN read comes from the UT-side getprop, which is the v63 stub's shape
  242:FAIL  so the reason it gives for the <=27 branch is biometryd's, not the device's
  318:FAIL  and it labels the container read as a cross-check, because biometryd does not read it
  394:FAIL  with the offline-known value (2026-06-07 vendor.img) next to it, so the two readings can be compared
  470:FAIL  and it says when the two independent readings agree, which is what makes the write safe
  556:FAIL  container says 29 but the UT getprop is the stub -> biometryd passes <=27, and that is the path created
  579:FAIL  NOT the path the container's value would suggest (the old probe's answer)
  581:FAIL  and it says the two readings disagree instead of silently picking one
  669:FAIL  it reports that the UT getprop answers (so the level is a real read, not a default)
  759:FAIL  and both readings agree here
  847:FAIL  the container reports the value the real vendor.img build.prop carries
  933:FAIL  and it says so explicitly
  pass=111 fail=13

   and 13 red is 13 sides of one defect: the shipped probe prints a level, an AGREES/DISAGREES verdict
   and a stated reason that all come from the container's read; the two that matter most are
     "container says 29 but the UT getprop is the stub -> biometryd passes <=27, and that is the path
      created"  and  "NOT the path the container's value would suggest (the old probe's answer)".

