6. what this round does NOT show
================================================================================
  * NOTHING ABOUT THE DEVICE. The device is in EDL; the probe has still never run on it, and the
    write (--create-store-dir) has never been made. The claim here is about which path it would
    create and why, settled from the images and the source.
  * The /data image is from 2026-09-16, before the netwatch, cpufreq and keeper work. It answers
    "did the directory exist on a device that had booted many times", not "is it absent right now".
  * first_api_level=23 is the VENDOR build.prop's value. It is what the container's property area
    would serve; it is not a getprop run on the device.
  * **Even with the directory created, nothing proves a fingerprint would then work.** Below
    access(W_OK) sit the innermost HAL's own store (/data/gf_data, also absent), the daemon (gx_fpd)
    and libQSEEComAPI.so. Doc 83's EINVAL was the FIRST of three silences; this round confirms the
    first two are real absences and says nothing about the third.
  * Creating the directory is a write to Android's /data and remains the user's call. The probe
    prints the undo for exactly the one path it created.

7. and a defect in the installer harness itself, found by re-running it after this commit
================================================================================
zl1-installers-selftest.sh went from pass=229/fail=0 to fail=3 the moment the cpufreq fix was
committed, without a line of the script under test changing. Its "and the applier that actually
shipped, on the very same fixture" scenario reads

    git show HEAD:scripts/install-cpufreq-governor.sh

and the whole point of that scenario is "the version that SHIPPED exits 0 while a core never moved".
Once the fix is in HEAD, HEAD *is* the fixed applier -- the scenario silently became "the fix,
compared against itself". The guard that was meant to catch that asked for the string

    did NOT take it

while the applier prints `did NOT take '<governor>'`, so it is in NEITHER version: an assertion that
could only ever pass, which is the exact defect this project keeps finding in the scripts this file
tests. Fixed by walking the file's history for the newest revision whose applier has NO read-back and
printing which revision that was:

    PASS  the pre-fix revision is 8a21b99c0c70, whose applier has no read-back

Two things came with it. `git log -- "$CP"` had to become a REPO-RELATIVE path, because the
documented way to run this harness is a copy of the tree placed outside the repository, and
`git log -- /tmp/copy/...` finds no history at all -- under the project's own instructions the first
version turned a working comparison into a hard error. And when no history is available it prints a
`SKIP` line, counts it separately and names it in the total, rather than quietly comparing the script
against itself.

The same stale-HEAD problem was then found in the WRITTEN reproduction commands of docs 99 and 100
(both said `git show HEAD:...`), which no longer reproduce their own recorded failures. All three are
now "copy the current tree, roll back only the scripts that round fixed to a named revision", and each
was re-run:

    docs 99   (revert install-retire-debug-keeper.sh to 55a490e)   pass=224 fail=5
    docs 100  (revert cpufreq + netwatch to 3a26b66)               pass=211 fail=15
    docs 101  (revert both probes to 25f7ba1)                      pass=111 fail=13

